files-reg: fall back to ghost files when the filesystem cannot hard-link

An open file deleted on NFS is silly-renamed to .nfsXXXX. CRIU dumps it as a
linked remap, which needs linkat(). Filesystems without hard links (Fusion,
a FUSE server re-exported over NFS) fail it with ENOTSUPP (524), and the whole
dump fails. Restore also links the remap back to its original name, so a
dump-only fallback would fail there instead.

- Dump: if linkat() says links are unsupported (EOPNOTSUPP, ENOTSUPP) for a
  silly-renamed file, copy its contents as a ghost file instead. Only for
  silly-renamed files: they go away with the task, so a copy is faithful. An
  unreachable path to a file that still has other links keeps failing, a copy
  would silently detach the task from that file.
- Restore: if linking the remap to its original name fails the same way, open
  the remap file directly, as is done for directories. try_clean_remaps()
  unlinks it afterwards. rfi_remap() returns 2 for that case, so it doesn't
  depend on a stale errno from an earlier failure.

See seqeralabs/fusion#1721.

